steps.text:
**Answer: Amazon (AMZN) plans to spend the most on capex in 2025, at roughly $100 billion.** Alphabet (GOOG) is second at about $75 billion, and Meta (META) is third at $60–65 billion. These are the guidance figures available as of April 7, 2025.

**Amazon (~$100B):** On its Q4 2024 call in February 2025, Amazon said it expects to raise capex to about $100 billion in 2025. 
The company reported $83 billion in capital expenditures last year.
 The figure includes more than AI infrastructure. 
The expected $100 billion annual capex, up from $83 billion last year, also includes e-commerce warehouses and offices.


**Alphabet (~$75B):** 
"We are confident about the opportunities ahead, and to accelerate our progress, we expect to invest approximately $75 billion in capital expenditures in 2025," Pichai said
 in the Q4 2024 earnings release, per CNBC.

**Meta ($60–65B):** In January 2025, 
Meta CEO Mark Zuckerberg announced the company plans to invest around $60 billion to $65 billion in capital expenditures in 2025
. Meta's SEC filing guidance at that time matched this range. 
"We anticipate our full year 2025 capital expenditures will be in the range of 60-65 billion dollars."


**Comparison (midpoints for reference):**
- Amazon: ~$100B
- Alphabet: ~$75B (about $25B less than Amazon)
- Meta: $60–65B, midpoint $62.5B (about $37.5B less than Amazon)

Amazon's figure is the highest even at the low end of the other two ranges. Note that Amazon's ~$100B is an annualized run-rate estimate from its Q4 capex of $26.3B rather than a formal range. Also, the guidance cited here predates later revisions. I've excluded any post-April 7, 2025 updates, since the question is framed as of that date.

